# gRPC Architecture Rules

## Project Structure

```
proto/                      # .proto source files (source of truth)
gen/                        # Generated Go code — committed, never hand-edited
    {package}/v1/
        {service}.pb.go
        {service}_grpc.pb.go
internal/
    service/                # Business logic — implements generated Server interface
        {service}.go
        {service}_test.go
    repository/             # Data access layer (interfaces + implementations)
cmd/server/
    main.go                 # Wire dependencies, register services, start server
buf.yaml                    # buf module config
buf.gen.yaml                # buf generation config
```

## Server Implementation
Implement the generated `{Service}Server` interface in `internal/service/`. Keep gRPC types out of service business logic — use domain types internally. Map between proto types and domain types at the handler layer (the generated server method implementations in the service file).

## Interceptors for Cross-Cutting Concerns
Auth, logging, rate limiting, and tracing go in gRPC interceptors (unary + stream interceptors). Never put cross-cutting concerns inside service business logic methods.

```go
s := grpc.NewServer(
    grpc.ChainUnaryInterceptor(
        loggingInterceptor,
        authInterceptor,
    ),
)
```

## Health Check
Every gRPC service MUST register the standard gRPC health check:

```go
import "google.golang.org/grpc/health/grpc_health_v1"
import "google.golang.org/grpc/health"

healthServer := health.NewServer()
healthServer.SetServingStatus("", grpc_health_v1.HealthCheckResponse_SERVING)
grpc_health_v1.RegisterHealthServer(s, healthServer)
```

Required for Kubernetes liveness and readiness probes. Non-negotiable.

## Server Reflection
Register `reflection.Register(s)` in non-production environments to enable grpcurl debugging. Gate it on an environment variable — never enable in production.

```go
if os.Getenv("GRPC_REFLECTION") == "true" {
    reflection.Register(s)
}
```

## Error Handling
Return gRPC status errors. Map domain errors to gRPC status codes consistently. Use a central error mapping function rather than inline `status.Errorf` everywhere.

```go
// Good: central mapping
func toGRPCError(err error) error {
    if errors.Is(err, ErrNotFound) {
        return status.Errorf(codes.NotFound, err.Error())
    }
    if errors.Is(err, ErrInvalidArgument) {
        return status.Errorf(codes.InvalidArgument, err.Error())
    }
    return status.Errorf(codes.Internal, "internal error")
}
```

Common mappings:
- `ErrNotFound` → `codes.NotFound`
- `ErrInvalidArgument` → `codes.InvalidArgument`
- `ErrAlreadyExists` → `codes.AlreadyExists`
- `ErrUnauthenticated` → `codes.Unauthenticated`
- `ErrPermissionDenied` → `codes.PermissionDenied`
- unexpected errors → `codes.Internal` (never expose internal details)

## Deadlines and Context
Always check `ctx.Err()` after long operations. Propagate deadlines — never ignore context cancellation. Context is the first argument to every function that may block or call downstream services.

```go
func (s *UserService) GetUser(ctx context.Context, req *pb.GetUserRequest) (*pb.GetUserResponse, error) {
    user, err := s.repo.FindByID(ctx, req.Id)
    if err != nil {
        if ctx.Err() != nil {
            return nil, status.FromContextError(ctx.Err()).Err()
        }
        return nil, toGRPCError(err)
    }
    return &pb.GetUserResponse{User: toProto(user)}, nil
}
```
